# I deleted something — how do I get it back?

Most "I lost a file" situations have a one-click answer that does
not need your operator. This page tells you which one to try first
and when to escalate.

## Step 1: try the app's own undelete

Each app keeps its own trash for 30 days by default. Check there
first — it is the fastest path and you do not need anyone's help.

| App | Where to look |
|---|---|
| Nextcloud | left sidebar → **Deleted files** |
| Outline | sidebar → **Trash** |
| EspoCRM | top-right gear → **Recycle Bin** |
| Plane | project sidebar → **Archived** (issues) / **Archive** (pages) |
| Rocket.Chat | message history is preserved per channel; deleted messages are recoverable by an admin via channel kebab → "Prune" → un-prune |
| n8n | execution history (deleted **workflows** are not recoverable from inside n8n; see step 2) |

## Step 2: contact your operator

If the app's trash has been emptied, or the app does not have
undelete (n8n workflows, certain settings panels), one nightly
backup ago still has it.

Send your operator:

1. **What** you deleted (file name, folder name, workflow name).
2. **Where** it lived (which app, which user account).
3. **When** you last saw it (today? yesterday? last week?).

Your operator can recover a single file from any past nightly
snapshot without taking the rest of your stack offline. The
recovery typically takes 5-15 minutes.

## What is and is not recoverable

| Lost thing | Recoverable from backup? |
|---|---|
| Files in Nextcloud (filesystem-stored) | Yes, up to the last 30 nightly snapshots |
| Files in Nextcloud (S3-stored, large deployments) | Recoverable from the **bucket's** versioning, not the VPS backup. Your operator restores from the S3 provider |
| Outline pages | Yes |
| EspoCRM records | Yes |
| Rocket.Chat messages | Yes (whole channel state) |
| n8n workflow JSON | Yes |
| n8n execution logs | Only the last 14 days — n8n prunes its own history |
| A user account / SSO seat | See [Disaster recovery](disaster-recovery.md) → "User access loss" |

## How big a window do I have?

Your nightly backup retention defaults to:

- 7 daily snapshots
- 4 weekly snapshots
- 3 monthly snapshots

So the file is recoverable as long as it existed within roughly
the last 90 days **and** was present in at least one of the
retained snapshots above. After that, the snapshot is pruned by
the retention policy and the file is gone.

Larger retention windows are available — ask your operator.

## When to ask your operator vs do it yourself

The app's own trash (step 1) is yours to use freely — no
coordination needed.

Recovery from a past snapshot (step 2) is operator-driven on
purpose. Restoring the wrong file or the wrong version onto a
live volume can confuse the running app; your operator follows a
short runbook that stages the recovery before it goes live, then
tells the app to re-scan its index. It is fast for them — under
fifteen minutes for one file in most cases — and it avoids the
"I tried to fix it myself and now nothing works" failure mode.
